## How to run it

### Phase 1 — Plan (single human gate)

```bash
claude
> /{{WF}} "{{PARAMS}}"
```

The command forces plan mode, researches read-only, and presents a plan (steps,
agents, files touched, commands, network/installs, scope). **Approving the plan is
the only gate.** On approval, Claude writes `.workflow/plan.md` (marked
`status: approved`) and `.workflow/allowlist.txt`, then stops.

### Phase 2 — Execute (autonomous)

`bypassPermissions` cannot be entered mid-session, so relaunch a fresh session:

```bash
claude --dangerously-skip-permissions        # fresh, clean context (recommended)
> /{{WF}}-execute
```

Alternative (keeps planning context, higher token cost):

```bash
claude --continue --dangerously-skip-permissions
> /{{WF}}-execute
```

The execute command runs a preflight (approved plan present? guard hook and deny
rules installed? bypass session?) and then runs the plan under the orchestrator.

## First-time setup

Before the first Phase 2 run, make sure the enforcement layer is installed in the
**target project**:

1. Copy `workflow-guard.sh` into `<project>/.claude/hooks/`.
2. Merge the deny rules and hook registration from the settings snippet into
   `<project>/.claude/settings.local.json`.
3. Install `jq` if the host lacks it. The guard parses hook JSON with it and
   **denies every guarded action** until it is present.
4. Restart Claude Code so the hooks load (hooks are read at session start).

These files are OS-neutral: the same guard and the same registration work on
macOS, Linux, and Windows, so they are safe to commit and clone across a mixed-OS
team. The one exception is a **native Windows host with no Git for Windows**,
which has no bash to run the guard. Either install
[Git for Windows](https://git-scm.com/downloads/win) (recommended — Claude Code
wants it for the Bash tool anyway), or swap that machine's local
`settings.local.json` to the bundled `workflow-guard.ps1`:

```json
{ "type": "command", "shell": "powershell",
  "command": "& \"${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}/.claude/hooks/workflow-guard.ps1\"" }
```

Keep that swap local — committing it breaks the guard for everyone else.

## Safety warning

`--dangerously-skip-permissions` disables permission prompts and most safety
checks and gives no protection against prompt injection. Per Anthropic's guidance,
**run Phase 2 only inside a container, VM, dev container, or a dedicated sandbox
directory.** Claude Code refuses this flag when running as root/sudo.

The workflow still constrains itself mechanically even under bypass:

- **Deny rules** (apply in every mode) block force push, `reset --hard`, branch
  deletion, `git clean`, writes to `~`, and reads of `~/.ssh` / `~/.aws`.
- **The guard hook** (runs in every mode) confines writes to the project +
  `.workflow/`, blocks `rm -rf` outside `.workflow/`, and blocks network/install
  commands unless they appear in `.workflow/allowlist.txt` (populated only from
  the approved plan).
- **Out-of-scope work** stops execution and writes `.workflow/replan-needed.md`
  rather than improvising — re-run `/{{WF}}` to re-plan.

## `.workflow/` layout

```
.workflow/
├── plan.md            # approved plan (status: approved)
├── allowlist.txt      # permitted network/install command patterns
├── state.md           # orchestrator checkpoint after each phase
├── log.md             # append-only execution log
├── replan-needed.md   # written iff execution hit out-of-scope work
└── steps/             # full worker outputs (workers return only summaries)
```

You can inspect any file in `.workflow/` mid-run to audit progress.
